---
import BaseLayout from '../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../../../components/navigation/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { buildLocalePath } from '../../../../i18n/utils';

const locale = 'en-US' as const;

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BlogPosting",
  "headline": "How to Ask for a Raise: Prepare, Time It, and Make Your Case",
  "description": "A raise request works best when it is prepared, timed well, and tied to your work: gather your contributions, check the range for your role, choose a calm moment, and ask for a specific outcome — including non-salary options if pay cannot move.",
  "datePublished": "2026-11-03",
  "dateModified": "2026-11-03",
  "author": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "PayRenTax"
  },
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "PayRenTax",
    "logo": {
      "@type": "ImageObject",
      "url": "https://payrentax.me/logo.svg"
    }
  },
  "inLanguage": "en-US",
  "keywords": "how to ask for a raise, salary negotiation, raise request, negotiate salary"
};
---

<BaseLayout
  locale={locale}
  title="How to Ask for a Raise: Prepare, Time It, and Make Your Case | PayRenTax"
  description="A raise request works best when it is prepared, timed well, and tied to your work: gather your contributions, check the range for your role, and ask for a specific outcome."
  canonical="/en-us/blog/how-to-ask-for-a-raise-2026/"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
  </Fragment>

  <Breadcrumbs
    locale={locale}
    trail={[
      { label: 'Blog', href: buildLocalePath(locale, 'blog') },
      { label: 'How to Ask for a Raise: Prepare, Time It, and Make Your Case', href: buildLocalePath(locale, 'blog/how-to-ask-for-a-raise-2026') }
    ]}
  />

  <article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-slate-900">How to Ask for a Raise: Prepare, Time It, and Make Your Case</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
      Most people put off asking for a raise because the conversation feels awkward. It
      gets easier when you treat it as a normal business discussion: you are not begging
      for a favour, you are presenting the case that your pay should match the work you
      do. Preparation does most of the work before you ever sit down.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Gather your case first</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
      Write down what has changed since your pay was last set: new responsibilities,
      projects you led, problems you solved, skills you added, and results you can point
      to. Specific examples beat general claims every time — "I took over the weekly
      reporting and cut the time it takes in half" is a case, while "I work hard" is not.
      Also check the typical range for your role and location, so your request is
      grounded in the market rather than in a number picked from thin air.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Time it deliberately</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
      Timing matters as much as content. The best moments are usually after a strong
      review, after a visible win, or during the period when budgets and salaries are
      actually being decided — not in the middle of a crisis week or the day after a
      difficult conversation. If your employer has a formal review cycle, align your
      request with it, and give your manager enough notice to make a case upwards rather
      than ambushing them with a deadline.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">Ask for a specific outcome</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
      Go in with a clear, specific ask rather than a vague "I would like more money."
      Name the figure or range you have in mind, and tie it to the case you prepared.
      Then listen: if the answer is no, ask what would need to change for the answer to
      become yes, and agree on a date to revisit it. A no with a clear path forward is
      far more useful than a vague promise to "see what can be done."
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900">If pay cannot move, negotiate the rest</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
      Salary is only one part of total compensation. If the pay budget is genuinely
      frozen, other things may still be movable: extra time off, a different schedule,
      training, a title change that supports the next raise, or a bonus tied to clear
      targets. Agree on anything you accept in writing, with a review date, so it does
      not quietly evaporate. And before you accept or decline, run the new figure
      through a paycheck calculator — a raise that looks good as a gross number is only
      real once you see what it adds to take-home pay.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-6">
      <a href="/en-us/salary/salary-raise/" class="font-semibold text-accent">Salary raise calculator</a>
      <span class="text-slate-400"> · </span>
      <a href="/en-us/salary/paycheck-calculator/" class="font-semibold text-accent">Paycheck calculator</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
